module Data.Bifunctor (Bifunctor(..), first, second)

import Prelude ()
import Std.Classes

-- Two type parameters, both mapped. `Either` is right-biased, so `Functor
-- (Either l)` can only reach the right; this is how the left is reached, and
-- reaching it is not an error operation -- `Either` is not `Result`, and
-- nothing here presumes a `Left` is a failure.
--
-- The class variable has kind `* -> * -> *`, discovered from `f a b` in
-- `bimap`'s own signature and written down nowhere, exactly as `Monad`'s
-- `* -> *` is.
--
-- The Prelude does not mention this module, in either tier: not in
-- `Std.Classes` among the names it puts in every file, and not among the ten
-- modules it re-exports qualified. `Functor`, `Applicative` and `Monad` are in
-- the first because `?` is written in terms of them; `Array`, `Int` and
-- `String` are in the second because everything reaches for them. Nothing in
-- the language names this one and most programs never touch it, so it does not
-- earn a permanent slot in either namespace -- a program that wants it imports
-- it, as it would any other library module.
--
-- Unchecked law: `second` must agree with `map` at every type that is both a
-- `Functor` (in its second parameter) and a `Bifunctor`.
class Bifunctor f {
    fun bimap(f a b, fun(a) -> c, fun(b) -> d) -> f c d
}

-- `Data.Bifunctor.first` in Haskell, and the operation `Control.Arrow.left`
-- also denotes -- `left` reaches it through `ArrowChoice` at `(->)`, which is
-- an accident of the arrow hierarchy rather than the thing meant.
fun first[Bifunctor f](x : f a b, g : fun(a) -> c) -> f c b =
    bimap(x, g, fun(y) = y)

fun second[Bifunctor f](x : f a b, h : fun(b) -> d) -> f a d =
    bimap(x, fun(y) = y, h)
